    "narrative": "LawDog\u2019s offensive outlook for August 23rd is anchored by **Lawrence Butler** and **Dylan Beavers**, our strongest starts of the day. **Lawrence Butler** (74.61) thrives in a favorable matchup against Javier, who carries a shaky 5.16 xERA; the combination of a high-leverage lineup spot and a significant platoon advantage makes him our primary anchor. Similarly, **Dylan Beavers** (69.52) benefits from a high-efficiency environment against Martinez, bolstered by a strong park factor and a 17% boost from the SP\u2019s xERA, making him a high-floor play in the outfield. **Jonathan Aranda** (68.94) also stands out, as he maximizes a rare +16% park factor and the platoon edge against Baz to comfortably exceed his base projection.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench today involves **Jung Hoo Lee** (50.65). Despite his elite sprint speed\u2014a critical asset given our focus on stealing bases\u2014his score was suppressed by a unfavorable platoon split against the lefty Bennett. While the optimizer considered his speed, it ultimately favored the higher projected efficiency of **Justin Crawford** (57.03) and **Gavin Sheets** (56.82). The latter is a marginal start, but his raw power projection outweighs the incremental value of **Jung Hoo Lee** in this specific pitching matchup. \n\nRisk management remains a priority, particularly for **Cal Raleigh** (55.95) and **Zack Gelof** (59.56). **Cal Raleigh** faces the toughest test of the day in Imanaga, whose 3.69 xERA presents a legitimate hurdle for the catcher, while **Zack Gelof** must contend with an elite-hold SP profile that actively threatens our stolen base potential. We are monitoring all lineups closely, as **Gleyber Torres** continues to provide solid value from the leadoff spot; however, should any late-inning weather concerns arise, we are prepared to pivot to our bench reserves to protect our remaining game caps.",
